<p id="p2">Yesterday, <a href="https://www.wired.com/story/apple-homeview-smart-display/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Wired reported</a> that third-party apps with CarPlay functionality would be available on Apple’s new HomeView device. That struck me as odd. I was expecting, and I think many others were at least hoping, that the HomeView would be a new platform for building apps. And while strictly speaking that doesn’t seem to be the case, in an entirely different way it is. Here’s what’s going on.</p>
<p id="p3"><a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/10/meet-apple-homeview-a-new-way-to-enjoy-entertainment-and-manage-the-home/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple’s original press release</a> caused some confusion because it doesn’t spell out the CarPlay angle. Instead, it simply says that the HomeView supports “compatible third-party audio and video apps,” and “[s]elect apps require a paired and compatible iPhone nearby,” which is also a CarPlay requirement. That press release also mentioned the HomeView’s ability to use widgets and access personal context without ever mentioning whether that applies to third-party apps.</p>
<p id="p4">Well, now we have some answers to most of these questions. First of all, <a href="https://developer.apple.com/carplay/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple documents</a> <a href="https://developer.apple.com/download/files/CarPlay-Developer-Guide.pdf" rel="noopener noreferrer">out-of-the-box support</a> for <a href="https://developer.apple.com/carplay/" rel="noopener noreferrer">audio and video apps</a>, rather than the full range of CarPlay categories. That’s further supported by <a href="https://youtu.be/kqgfdR9jW4A?si=N2cuaxZQx2lTmc0w" rel="noopener noreferrer">Katarina Mogus’ interview with Apple’s Cecilia Dantas</a>, which draws a straight line between third-party video apps and CarPlay support.</p>
<p id="p5">Second, the <a href="https://developer.apple.com/design/human-interface-guidelines/widgets#Apple-HomeView" rel="noopener noreferrer">Human Interface Guidelines have been updated</a> to address which widgets can be used with the HomeView:</p>
<blockquote id="blockquote6"><p>
  On Apple HomeView, people can place widgets on the Modular face and the Home Screen. Widgets on HomeView are from the small system family, and share the appearance of iPhone widgets. A person chooses widgets from among those already installed on HomeView, like Weather or World Clock, or from their individually connected iPad or iPhone.
</p></blockquote>
<p id="p7">As for whether Siri can access third-party data and actions, I suspect the answer is yes. <a href="https://www.apple.com/newsroom/2026/10/meet-apple-homeview-a-new-way-to-enjoy-entertainment-and-manage-the-home/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Apple’s press release</a> called out personal context specifically, which was confirmed by Apple’s Kaiann Drance and Lilian Rincon in <a href="https://www.reuters.com/business/retail-consumer/apples-homeview-brings-personalized-siri-smart-home-takes-amazon-google-2026-10-13/" rel="noopener noreferrer">an interview with Reuters</a>. So, for example, you can presumably use the HomeView to retrieve an old message from the Messages app on your iPhone. The <a href="https://www.apple.com/apple-homeview/" rel="noopener noreferrer">Q&amp;A section on the HomeView product page</a> also mentions taking action across apps with Siri. That strongly suggests that the HomeView’s functionality will be the same as the new HomePod mini 2’s when it gains Siri AI capabilities, even though I haven’t seen anything from Apple that addresses whether that sort of Siri integration extends to third-party apps directly.</p>
<p id="p8">So, as of today, Apple has documented support for CarPlay audio and video apps and small widgets. I suspect Siri AI will provide another route, too. That limited app model may disappoint some, but I think it points to where personal assistants are heading. Apps aren’t going away, but the need for a separate app on every device is.</p>
<p id="p9">Siri, with apps integrated through App Intents, is emerging as a new UI. Developers who have adopted that technology already give users ways to interact with their apps beyond the apps themselves. I expect HomeView will extend that opportunity even further without requiring developers to build for an entirely new device. If it does, that’s a win for developers and users alike, and perhaps just the tip of the iceberg in a world where computing extends beyond apps.</p>
